:pnlgrp.
.************************************************************************
.*  Help for command OPNPIPE
.************************************************************************
:help name='OPNPIPE'.
Open Pipe & Run Unix Cmd - Help
:p.The Open Pipe & Run Unix Cmd (OPNPIPE) command starts a
process in either the QShell or PASE environment (from here forward,
referred to as "Unix" process) and connects that Unix process
to your CL program using pipes.
.*
:p.A pipe is an interprocess communication tool. Think of it the
way you'd think of a data queue. Or in a more abstract sense,
the way you'd think of a water pipe. Any data written to the pipe
on one end of the connection will be received on the other end
of the connection (and vice versa.) This is analogous to the way
water poured into one end of a pipe will come out the other end.
.*
:p.Once the process is started and connected using the OPNPIPE
command, you'll be able to write data to the pipe using the Send
to Pipe (SNDPIPE) command. To receive responses from the Unix
process, use the Receive from Pipe (RCVPIPE) command. To stop
the Unix process, close the pipes, and check whether the command
succeeded, use the Close Pipe (CLOPIPE) command.
.*
:p.:hp2.Restrictions::ehp2.
:ul.
:li.
You must have *X authority to each directory in the path name
that precedes the Unix command you are to run.
:li.
If the command is a compiled executable, you must have *X authority
to that executable.
:li.
If the command is a shell script, you must have *RX authority
to the shell script.
:li.
The command that you're running may impose additional restrictions
on whether you can run it.
:li.
Adopted authority is not usually supported in the IFS.  Since Unix
tools typically use the IFS, you will not be able to gain the above
authorities by adopting them.
:eul.
:ehelp.
.*******************************************
.*   Help for parameter CMD
.*******************************************
:help name='OPNPIPE/CMD'.
Command to run (CMD) - Help
:xh3.Command to run (CMD)
:p.Specifies the QShell or PASE command line that you'd like to run.
This command is invoked by the QShell command interpreter, or the
PASE /QOpenSys/usr/bin/sh shell.
:p.Since the command is run by the shell, you can use redirection,
pipelines, environment variables, and so forth as you would normally
use when running a command in a Unix shell. (Such as when running
the command interactively.)
.* Describe the function provided by the parameter.
:p.This is a required parameter.
:parml.
:pt.:pv.character-value:epv.
:pd.
Specify the command to run.
:eparml.
:ehelp.
.*******************************************
.*   Help for parameter TYPE
.*******************************************
:help name='OPNPIPE/TYPE'.
Command environment (TYPE) - Help
:xh3.Command environment (TYPE)
:p.Specifies the environment in which to invoke the command.
:parml.
:pt.:pk def.*QSHELL:epk.
:pd.
The command will be interpreted and run by the QShell interpreter.
This is equivalent to running your command with the STRQSH tool,
except that it will be connected to your CL program with a pipe.
.*
:pt.:pk.*PASE:epk.
:pd.
The command will be interpreted and run by the /QOpenSys/usr/bin/sh
program provided by the PASE environment. This is equivalent to
calling the QP2SHELL API and specifying /QOpenSys/usr/bin/sh as the
first parameter, except that the process will be connected to your
CL program with a pipe.
:eparml.
:ehelp.
.*******************************************
.*   Help for parameter PIPEID
.*******************************************
:help name='OPNPIPE/PIPEID'.
CL VAR for Pipe ID (PIPEID) - Help
:xh3.CL VAR for Pipe ID (PIPEID)
:p.(Optional) This allows you to specify a variable that will
receive a Pipe ID. The Pipe ID is useful when you wish to have
more than one Unix process running simultaneously. Each time
you open a new pipe, a PipeID will be assigned.  You can use the
PipeID to tell the SNDPIPE, RCVPIPE or CLOPIPE commands which
pipe to operate on.
:parml.
:pt.:pv.decimal-number:epv.
:pd.
Specify the CL variable that will receive the Pipe ID.  If you
do not use Pipe IDs, the SNDPIPE, RCVPIPE and CLOPIPE commands
will always act on the most recently opened pipe.
:eparml.
:ehelp.
.**************************************************
.*
.* Examples for OPNPIPE
.*
.**************************************************
:help name='OPNPIPE/COMMAND/EXAMPLES'.
Examples for OPNPIPE - Help
:xh3.Examples for OPNPIPE
:p.:hp2.Example 1: Simple Command Example:ehp2.
:xmp.
OPNPIPE CMD('cd /tmp && ls')
RCVPIPE RCD(&FILENAME) EOF(&EOF)
DOWHILE (&EOF *EQ '0')
   /* Do processing on &FILENAME */
   RCVPIPE RCD(&FILENAME) EOF(&EOF)
ENDDO
CLOPIPE
:exmp.
:p.This example invokes the QShell 'cd' command to switch to the
/tmp IFS directory. It then proceeds to run the QShell 'ls' command
to get a list of files in that directory. The Unix process that
runs these commands is connected to the CL program with a pipe.
:p.When you receive data from the Unix process using the RCVPIPE
command, it reads one line of the output of the 'ls' command
into the &FILENAME variable. You can use this to get a list of
files in the /tmp IFS directory.
.*
:p.:hp2.Example 2: More Complex Command Example:ehp2.
:xmp.
OPNPIPE CMD('cd /tmp && ls old*.dat') PIPEID(&LSPIPE)
RCVPIPE RCD(&FILENAME) EOF(&EOF) PIPEID(&LSPIPE)
DOWHILE (&EOF *EQ '0')
   OPNPIPE CMD('cd /tmp && rm' *BCAT &FILENAME) PIPEID(&RMPIPE)
   CLOPIPE PIPEID(&RMPIPE)
   RCVPIPE RCD(&FILENAME) EOF(&EOF) PIPEID(&LSPIPE)
ENDDO
CLOPIPE PIPEID(&LSPIPE)
:exmp.
:p.This command starts a QShell process that reads the name of
the files in the /tmp directory of the IFS, just as the previous
example did, except that it only reads files that match a
wildcard pattern of old*.dat.
:p.For each filename that matches that pattern, it starts a new ,
pipe (therefore having two simultaneous invocations of QShell) and
uses that new process to delete the file with the 'rm' (remove)
QShell command.
.*
:p.:hp2.Example 3: Invoking a command via PASE:ehp2.
:xmp.
OPNPIPE CMD('ssh example.com cd /tmp "&&" ls new*') +
        TYPE(*PASE)
RCVPIPE RCD(&FILENAME) EOF(&EOF)
DOWHILE (&EOF *EQ '0')
   /* Process filename */
   RCVPIPE RCD(&FILENAME) EOF(&EOF)
ENDDO
CLOPIPE
:exmp.
:p.This example uses the *PASE environment instead of QShell.
It runs the 'ssh' command (provided with IBM licpgm 57xx-SC1) to
connect to a separate computer named example.com.  On that computer,
it runs the 'ls' command to list files that match a pattern
of new*.
:p.The remote computer returns the file names to the pipe, and they
are read into your CL program using the RCVPIPE command.
:ehelp.
